Managing Variables and Facts
Objectives
Design an Ansible variable architecture that demonstrates precedence across play-level, group, host, and extra-var scopes
Build a fact-gathering system that collects, filters, and exposes both native and custom Ansible facts
Implement conditional playbook logic that adapts task execution based on live system facts and environment-specific variable values
Prerequisites
Comfort navigating and editing files on a Linux command line
Familiarity with YAML syntax and basic Ansible concepts such as playbooks, inventory, and modules
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Install and Verify the Ansible Environment
Requirement 1: Install Python 3, pip, and Ansible on the bare Ubuntu instance, then verify that the ansible and ansible-playbook binaries are reachable from your shell path and report a version string when invoked.

sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip
pip3 install --user ansible
echo 'export PATH=$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc && source ~/.bashrc
ansible --version
ansible-playbook --version
Official installation reference if the above fails: https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/installation_guide/intro_installation.html

Troubleshoot this step:

You see command not found: ansible after sourcing .bashrc.
Run python3 -m site --user-base to confirm the base path, then verify ~/.local/bin exists and contains the ansible binary; if the directory is different, update the PATH export to match.
https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/installation_guide/intro_installation.html
Requirement 2: Create a working directory at ~/ansible-lab5 containing an inventory/ subdirectory and a hosts file that defines a [local] group with localhost ansible_connection=local; confirm Ansible can ping that group with zero failures.

You should observe localhost | SUCCESS with "ping": "pong" in the output before proceeding.

Task 2: Design a Multi-Layer Variable Architecture
Problem statement: Design and implement a variable system that proves Ansible's precedence order by defining the same variable (log_level) at four distinct scopes â group vars, host vars, play vars, and extra-vars â then printing the resolved value at runtime so the winning scope is unambiguous.

Requirement 1: Create a directory layout containing at minimum group_vars/, host_vars/, and at least two playbooks; each scope must set log_level to a unique, clearly different string value (for example "group-scope", "host-scope", "play-scope", "extravars-scope"), and a single debug task must print the resolved value so you can confirm which scope wins when the playbook runs with and without -e log_level=extravars-scope.

Acceptance criteria:

Running the playbook without -e prints the host-var value, confirming host vars override group vars and play vars.
Running the playbook with -e log_level=extravars-scope prints extravars-scope, confirming extra-vars are the highest-precedence scope.
Requirement 2: Extend the same playbook to define a deployment_config dictionary variable at play level containing at minimum the keys environment, version, and replicas; a second playbook must load an external YAML file (passed via -e @filename) that overrides only deployment_config.environment and deployment_config.replicas while leaving version unchanged, and a debug task must print all three keys after the merge so the partial override is visible.

Acceptance criteria:

The printed output shows version retaining its play-level value while environment and replicas reflect the external file values.
The playbook exits with failed=0 in both the default run and the -e @file run.
Task 3: Build a Fact-Driven Conditional Deployment Playbook
Problem statement: Design and implement a single playbook that first gathers a targeted subset of system facts (OS family, total memory in MB, number of CPU cores, and all mount points), then uses those live facts to drive conditional task blocks that simulate environment-appropriate deployment decisions â without hard-coding any hostname or IP address anywhere in the playbook.

Requirement 1: Create a custom fact file at /etc/ansible/facts.d/app_meta.fact (an executable shell script that emits valid JSON) exposing at minimum the keys app_version, deploy_env, and max_workers; the playbook must gather facts selectively using the setup module's filter parameter (fact filtering means requesting only specific fact namespaces rather than the full fact set) to retrieve hardware and OS facts plus local custom facts, then print all six values â four native facts and two custom facts â in a single debug message.

Acceptance criteria:

The playbook runs with gather_facts: no at the play level, proving selective gathering via explicit setup tasks is the only source of facts.
The debug output contains non-empty, non-"Not available" values for ansible_distribution, ansible_memtotal_mb, ansible_processor_cores, ansible_mounts, ansible_local.app_meta.app_version, and ansible_local.app_meta.deploy_env.
Requirement 2: Add conditional block sections to the same playbook so that: a memory check block runs a "low-memory warning" debug task only when total RAM is below 2048 MB and a "sufficient memory" debug task only when RAM is at or above 2048 MB; an OS-family block runs Debian-specific debug tasks only on Debian-family systems and RedHat-specific debug tasks only on RedHat-family systems; and a disk-space block iterates over all mount points and prints a warning for any mount where available space is less than 20 percent of total size.

Acceptance criteria:

Exactly one of the two memory tasks fires per run, never both, verified by inspecting the skipping lines in verbose output (-v).
The disk-space loop produces at least one line of output (warning or clear) for the root mount /, confirming the loop iterates real mount data rather than an empty list.
Expected Outcomes
You will have a fully functional multi-scope variable system that visibly demonstrates Ansible precedence order through runtime output alone, with no ambiguity about which scope wins.
You will have a fact-driven playbook that adapts its task execution to the live state of the EC2 instance without any hard-coded system values, proving the playbook is portable across different host configurations.
Conclusion
This lab required you to reason about Ansible's variable precedence model and fact-gathering pipeline as design problems rather than configuration exercises. Selective fact gathering and conditional blocks are the primary tools for writing playbooks that remain correct across heterogeneous infrastructure. Mastering these patterns is a prerequisite for building production-grade automation that does not require manual adjustment when target systems differ.
